          The best generator oil is the grade your manual lists for the temperature you will run in: 10W-30 for most machines, 5W-30 synthetic for cold garages and winter storms, and SAE 30 for generators that only run in heat. For long outages, full synthetic buys you margin. Below are seven real oils and kits, and the rules for choosing between them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="which-oil" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Oil Does a Generator Take? 10W-30 for Most, With Temperature Exceptions
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most portable and inverter generators take SAE 10W-30, and that is the grade printed on the oil chart of most owner's manuals for general use. Some manuals list SAE 30 as the preferred oil in warm weather and 5W-30 for cold. The right generator oil is the grade your manual lists for the temperature you will be running in, in a detergent oil carrying a current API service classification. Everything else on the bottle is secondary.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two numbers in a multigrade oil describe two temperatures. The first number with the W (for winter) describes how the oil flows when cold; a lower number means it reaches the top of the engine faster on a cold start. The second number describes its thickness at operating temperature. A 5W-30 and a 10W-30 are the same thickness once the engine is hot; the 5W simply flows better at start-up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">10W-30: The Default for Most Generators</h3>

          <p>
            10W-30 covers the widest temperature range most households will see, from a cool spring exercise run to a long summer outage, which is why Honda, Champion and many other makers list it for general use. If you only ever buy one oil for the generator and you live somewhere without hard winters, buy a 10W-30. In very hot weather it runs slightly thinner than a straight 30, and some makers note that it can lead to slightly higher oil consumption above about 80°F, so check the level more often in summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">SAE 30: Warm Weather Only</h3>

          <p>
            Straight SAE 30 is a single-grade oil with no cold rating. Many small-engine manuals list it as the preferred oil above roughly 40°F, and it holds its thickness well when an air-cooled engine runs hot for hours. The catch is cold starts: below about 40°F it is thick enough to make a recoil start hard and to starve the top of the engine for those first critical seconds. It suits generators in hurricane country that only ever run in heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5W-30: Cold Garages and Winter Storms</h3>

          <p>
            5W-30, especially a full synthetic, is the grade for cold climates and for generators stored in unheated garages and sheds. Ice storms and blizzards are among the most common reasons for long outages, and that is exactly when a generator has to start cold. Many manuals list synthetic 5W-30 as acceptable across the whole temperature range, which makes it the most forgiving single choice if your manual allows it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diesel and Standby Units Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            Liquid-cooled standby generators and diesel portables follow their own oil charts, often with different classifications and grades such as 5W-20 for some Generac standby engines. Do not carry a portable-generator habit over to them; read the manual or the label inside the enclosure door.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="synthetic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Synthetic vs Conventional Generator Oil: Synthetic Wins for Long Outage Runs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Full synthetic oil is the better choice for a generator that may run for days through an outage, and conventional oil is perfectly adequate for one that runs a few hours a year and gets its oil changed on schedule. The difference is margin. A portable generator is an air-cooled engine running at a fixed, often heavy load, and it runs hotter than a car engine with a radiator. Heat is what breaks oil down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Heat Matters More in a Generator</h3>

          <p>
            An air-cooled generator relies on fins and a fan to shed heat, and the oil does a share of the cooling as it circulates. During a summer outage, with the generator carrying a refrigerator, freezer, sump pump and window air conditioner, oil temperatures climb. Conventional oil oxidizes and thickens faster under that heat, forms deposits and burns off more quickly. A full synthetic resists that breakdown for longer, which matters when you are adding hours faster than you can change oil.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Conventional Oil Is Fine</h3>

          <p>
            If the generator is exercised monthly for twenty minutes and runs perhaps twenty hours a year, conventional or synthetic-blend oil is fine, because it will be changed on the calendar long before it wears out on hours. The oil change guide's rule of hours or a year, whichever comes first, protects the engine more than the base oil does. Spend the money on changing it on time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Synthetic Blend: The Middle Ground</h3>

          <p>
            Synthetic blends mix synthetic and conventional base oils. They cost a little more than conventional and offer some of the cold-flow and heat resistance of a full synthetic. They make sense for light-use generators in moderate climates. For heavy outage duty, step up to a full synthetic.
          </p>

