          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most generators run best on 10W-30, with 5W-30 synthetic for cold garages and SAE 30 for warm-weather-only machines. Our top pick, Briggs & Stratton 100074 Full Synthetic 5W-30, covers the widest range of engines and seasons, while TRIAX 10W-30 is the oil to choose if your generator carries the house through long outages.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, the habits matter more than the label: match the grade to your manual, make the early break-in change, check the dipstick at every refuel and change the oil at least once a year. Keep a spare bottle next to the fuel and the generator will be ready when the lights go out.
          </p>
